# Line endings, where they are a correctness property rather than a style one.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the norm on Windows, and this repository had no
# attributes file, so every `*.sh` here was stored LF in the index and written
# CRLF into a Windows working tree. Real bash then refuses the file at its
# second line -- `set: pipefail\r: invalid option name` -- before a single
# assertion runs. Nobody on Windows could execute the shell suites at all,
# which is how `tests/test-todo-claims.sh` went its whole life unrun.
#
# `eol=lf` is checkout behaviour, so it fixes the working tree that `text`
# alone would not: `text` normalises on the way in, and the index was already
# clean. The rule is scoped to shell scripts on purpose -- `*.ps1` and the rest
# stay on the platform default, because CRLF costs them nothing.
#
# `tests/test_line_ending_conformance.py` fails if a tracked shell script ever
# falls outside these patterns, including one named without a `.sh` suffix.
*.sh text eol=lf
